## Proof

**Setup.** Let $\xi_n = e^{2\pi i/n}$ be a primitive $n$-th root of unity. The field $\mathbb{Q}[\xi_n]$ is the $n$-th cyclotomic field, a Galois extension of $\mathbb{Q}$ of degree $\phi(n)$ (Euler's totient). Every $\mathbb{Q}$-automorphism of $\mathbb{Q}[\xi_n]$ is determined by its action on $\xi_n$, and it must send $\xi_n$ to another primitive $n$-th root of unity, i.e., to $\xi_n^\ell$ with $\gcd(\ell,n)=1$. The automorphisms are exactly
$$\sigma_\ell:\ \xi_n \mapsto \xi_n^\ell,\qquad \ell\in(\mathbb{Z}/n\mathbb{Z})^\times.$$

**Isomorphism with the unit group.** The map
$$\Phi:\operatorname{Gal}(\mathbb{Q}[\xi_n]/\mathbb{Q})\longrightarrow(\mathbb{Z}/n\mathbb{Z})^\times,\qquad \sigma_\ell\mapsto \ell\bmod n$$
is a group isomorphism. Indeed it is bijective (every unit $\ell$ gives a valid automorphism, and distinct units give distinct automorphisms since they act differently on $\xi_n$), and it is a homomorphism because
$$\sigma_{\ell_1}\circ\sigma_{\ell_2}(\xi_n)=\sigma_{\ell_1}(\xi_n^{\ell_2})=(\xi_n^{\ell_1})^{\ell_2}=\xi_n^{\ell_1\ell_2}=\sigma_{\ell_1\ell_2}(\xi_n),$$
so $\Phi(\sigma_{\ell_1}\circ\sigma_{\ell_2})=\ell_1\ell_2=\Phi(\sigma_{\ell_1})\Phi(\sigma_{\ell_2})$.

**Identifying a generator.** By hypothesis the Galois group is cyclic, hence $(\mathbb{Z}/n\mathbb{Z})^\times$ is cyclic of order $\phi(n)$. A generator of a cyclic group of order $\phi(n)$ is an element of order exactly $\phi(n)$. Under the isomorphism $\Phi^{-1}$, such an element $\ell$ corresponds to the automorphism $\sigma_\ell$ whose powers
$$\sigma_\ell,\ \sigma_\ell^2,\ \dots,\ \sigma_\ell^{\phi(n)}=\mathrm{id}$$
exhaust the entire Galois group.

**Definition.** An integer $g$ with $\gcd(g,n)=1$ whose multiplicative order modulo $n$ equals $\phi(n)$ is called a **primitive root modulo $n$**. Such $g$ exists precisely when $(\mathbb{Z}/n\mathbb{Z})^\times$ is cyclic, i.e., when $n\in\{1,2,4,p^k,2p^k: p\text{ odd prime},\, k\ge1\}$ — which is exactly the case assumed in the problem.

**Conclusion.** The generator of the cyclic Galois group $\operatorname{Gal}(\mathbb{Q}[\xi_n]/\mathbb{Q})$ is

$$\boxed{\sigma_g:\ \xi_n\mapsto\xi_n^{g},}$$

where $g$ is any **primitive root modulo $n$**, i.e., any integer $g$ with $\gcd(g,n)=1$ and multiplicative order $\operatorname{ord}_n(g)=\phi(n)$. The automorphism $\sigma_g$ generates the whole group because $\sigma_g^k=\sigma_{g^k}$ runs through all $\sigma_\ell$ ($\ell\in(\mathbb{Z}/n\mathbb{Z})^\times$) as $k=0,1,\dots,\phi(n)-1$, since $g$ generates $(\mathbb{Z}/n\mathbb{Z})^\times$.

### PROOF COMPLETE
